Relevant tables & columns for this task  

• customer_transactions.customer_id : links every transaction to a unique customer.  
• customer_transactions.txn_date     : lets us isolate the year 2020 and derive calendar months.  
• customer_transactions.txn_type     : tells whether the row is a ‘deposit’ (+) or ‘withdrawal’ (–).  
• customer_transactions.txn_amount   : the quantity to add (deposit) or subtract (withdrawal).  

Typical filter / join conditions that will be needed  

• WHERE strftime('%Y', txn_date)= '2020'              -- keep only 2020 rows  
• CASE WHEN txn_type='deposit' THEN  txn_amount  
       WHEN txn_type='withdrawal' THEN –txn_amount END  -- signed value  
• GROUP BY customer_id, strftime('%m', txn_date)       -- build month‑end balances  
• HAVING SUM(signed_amount) > 0                        -- positive balances  
• ORDER BY positive_customer_cnt DESC / ASC            -- find highest / lowest month  

Below are ten exploratory‑to‑solution SQL queries (all limited to 20 rows).  
Each one is preceded by a brief description.

```sql
-- 1. Description: Inspect all distinct transaction types that exist in the data set.
SELECT DISTINCT txn_type
FROM customer_transactions
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: List distinct calendar months present in 2020 (formatted as 'YYYY‑MM').
SELECT DISTINCT strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date) AS month_2020
FROM customer_transactions
WHERE strftime('%Y', txn_date) = '2020'
ORDER BY month_2020
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: Show a sample of raw 2020 transactions with their signed amounts
--                 (deposit = +amount, withdrawal = –amount).
SELECT customer_id,
       txn_date,
       txn_type,
       txn_amount,
       CASE WHEN txn_type='deposit'     THEN  txn_amount
            WHEN txn_type='withdrawal'  THEN -txn_amount
       END AS signed_amount
FROM customer_transactions
WHERE strftime('%Y', txn_date)='2020'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: Compute month‑end balance per customer for January 2020 only.
SELECT customer_id,
       '2020-01'            AS month,
       SUM(CASE WHEN txn_type='deposit'     THEN  txn_amount
                WHEN txn_type='withdrawal'  THEN -txn_amount
           END)              AS month_end_balance
FROM customer_transactions
WHERE strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date)='2020-01'
GROUP BY customer_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: Compute month‑end balance for every customer & month in 2020.
SELECT customer_id,
       strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date) AS month,
       SUM(CASE WHEN txn_type='deposit'     THEN  txn_amount
                WHEN txn_type='withdrawal'  THEN -txn_amount
           END)                    AS month_end_balance
FROM customer_transactions
WHERE strftime('%Y', txn_date)='2020'
GROUP BY customer_id,
         strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: For each 2020 month, count customers whose month‑end balance is positive.
WITH month_balances AS (
    SELECT customer_id,
           strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date) AS month,
           SUM(CASE WHEN txn_type='deposit' THEN  txn_amount
                    WHEN txn_type='withdrawal' THEN -txn_amount
               END) AS month_end_balance
    FROM customer_transactions
    WHERE strftime('%Y', txn_date)='2020'
    GROUP BY customer_id, strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date)
)
SELECT month,
       COUNT(CASE WHEN month_end_balance > 0 THEN 1 END) AS positive_customer_cnt
FROM month_balances
GROUP BY month
ORDER BY month
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: Identify the month with the highest count of customers 
--                 having a positive month‑end balance.
WITH month_balances AS (
    SELECT customer_id,
           strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date) AS month,
           SUM(CASE WHEN txn_type='deposit' THEN  txn_amount
                    WHEN txn_type='withdrawal' THEN -txn_amount
               END) AS month_end_balance
    FROM customer_transactions
    WHERE strftime('%Y', txn_date)='2020'
    GROUP BY customer_id, strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date)
), positive_counts AS (
    SELECT month,
           COUNT(*) AS positive_customer_cnt
    FROM month_balances
    WHERE month_end_balance > 0
    GROUP BY month
)
SELECT month, positive_customer_cnt
FROM positive_counts
ORDER BY positive_customer_cnt DESC, month
LIMIT 1;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: Identify the month with the lowest count of customers 
--                 having a positive month‑end balance.
WITH month_balances AS (
    SELECT customer_id,
           strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date) AS month,
           SUM(CASE WHEN txn_type='deposit' THEN  txn_amount
                    WHEN txn_type='withdrawal' THEN -txn_amount
               END) AS month_end_balance
    FROM customer_transactions
    WHERE strftime('%Y', txn_date)='2020'
    GROUP BY customer_id, strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date)
), positive_counts AS (
    SELECT month,
           COUNT(*) AS positive_customer_cnt
    FROM month_balances
    WHERE month_end_balance > 0
    GROUP BY month
)
SELECT month, positive_customer_cnt
FROM positive_counts
ORDER BY positive_customer_cnt ASC, month
LIMIT 1;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: Calculate the average month‑end balance for the month 
--                 with the highest positive‑customer count.
WITH month_balances AS (
    SELECT customer_id,
           strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date) AS month,
           SUM(CASE WHEN txn_type='deposit' THEN  txn_amount
                    WHEN txn_type='withdrawal' THEN -txn_amount
               END) AS month_end_balance
    FROM customer_transactions
    WHERE strftime('%Y', txn_date)='2020'
    GROUP BY customer_id, strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date)
), positive_counts AS (
    SELECT month,
           COUNT(*) AS positive_customer_cnt
    FROM month_balances
    WHERE month_end_balance > 0
    GROUP BY month
), top_month AS (
    SELECT month
    FROM positive_counts
    ORDER BY positive_customer_cnt DESC, month
    LIMIT 1
)
SELECT mb.month,
       AVG(mb.month_end_balance) AS avg_month_end_balance
FROM month_balances AS mb
JOIN top_month        AS tm ON mb.month = tm.month
GROUP BY mb.month
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: Produce the final figure requested – the absolute difference
--                  between the average balances of the “highest” and “lowest”
--                  months identified above.
WITH month_balances AS (
    SELECT customer_id,
           strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date) AS month,
           SUM(CASE WHEN txn_type='deposit' THEN  txn_amount
                    WHEN txn_type='withdrawal' THEN -txn_amount
               END) AS month_end_balance
    FROM customer_transactions
    WHERE strftime('%Y', txn_date)='2020'
    GROUP BY customer_id, strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date)
), positive_counts AS (
    SELECT month,
           COUNT(*) AS positive_customer_cnt
    FROM month_balances
    WHERE month_end_balance > 0
    GROUP BY month
), high_month AS (
    SELECT month
    FROM positive_counts
    ORDER BY positive_customer_cnt DESC, month
    LIMIT 1
), low_month AS (
    SELECT month
    FROM positive_counts
    ORDER BY positive_customer_cnt ASC, month
    LIMIT 1
), avg_balances AS (
    SELECT month,
           AVG(month_end_balance) AS avg_bal
    FROM month_balances
    WHERE month IN (SELECT month FROM high_month
                    UNION ALL
                    SELECT month FROM low_month)
    GROUP BY month
)
SELECT MAX(CASE WHEN month = (SELECT month FROM high_month) THEN avg_bal END)
       - MIN(CASE WHEN month = (SELECT month FROM low_month)  THEN avg_bal END)
       AS avg_balance_difference
FROM avg_balances
LIMIT 20;
```

These ten queries move step‑by‑step from basic exploration of transaction types up to the complete calculation of the requested difference in average month‑end balances.
